   145	
   146	## Rules for every cache
   147	
   148	These apply to whichever cache is built. They come from how the researched tools fail.
   149	
   150	- **Full rebuild, never patched.** A live, incrementally updated index drifted silently from a clean rebuild: 4.3% of
   151	  edges wrong, with every integrity check passing (`G-F5 drift`). Its makers decided drift cannot be measured cheaply
   152	  (`G-F5c`). An FTS rebuild of 10,000 synthetic entries takes 0.53 s (`S-F13`).
   153	- **Rebuild before answering when stale.** Before each query, compare the recorded content hash of the
   154	  sources with the index and rebuild if they differ (`S-F11`: the hash; `S-F9`: rebuild before answering, though its
   155	  own check is the newest mtime, K1.1). Build into a temporary file and rename it over the old one (`S-F9`, quoted:
   156	  *"A stale index is worse than no index."*).
   157	- **Fall back when absent.** If the cache file or SQLite's FTS5 is missing, the tool answers with grep and says so in
   158	  every answer (Rd3.2) (`S-F9`); a missing embedding model falls back to full-text if it is there, and to grep if not
   159	  (Rd3.3; my reading, extending `S-F9`). A mistyped database path silently creates an empty database, which looks
   160	  exactly like a stale one (`G-F5 drift`, failure mode 5), so the tool opens the cache read-only and treats "no such
   161	  file" as absent.
   162	- **Index only active entries** — for `project-memory/` — by their own headers (B4.1), the set `INDEX.md` is
   163	  generated from, with `covers` stored
   164	  beside each row (`V-F22`, my reading). Otherwise superseded entries come back looking current.
   165	
   166	## SQLite full-text index
   167	
   168	### Five whys
   169	
   170	1. **Why a full-text index?** To find entries by their words, ranked, with a snippet, instead of grep's unranked list
   171	   (`S-F1`, quoted: *"grep returns unranked matches."*).
   172	2. **Why does ranking matter?** Every hit the agent opens costs context. In the one controlled study, BM25 used 5.8K
   173	   tokens per question against 226K for an agent using grep over files, on its smallest corpus: 1,144 documents,
   174	   1.7M tokens (`S-F4`).
   175	3. **Why not build it now?** That study puts the point where BM25 overtakes the grep agent at around 10 million tokens
   176	   of text, and the grep agent led at the smallest sizes (`S-F4`). At our entry size that is about 37,000 entries
   177	   (`S-F5`, my reading via its 4-characters-per-token assumption). `INDEX.md` already lists every active entry. In the
   178	   researcher's own test, at 10,000 synthetic entries a one-word grep took 0.038 s and a grep chain for a header
   179	   question 0.06 s; at 50,000 the header chain took 0.15 s, and the one-word timing was invalid (`S-F13`). No study
   180	   covers a corpus of a few hundred to a few thousand short entries (`S-F5`: not measured).
   181	4. **Why might it be needed later?** If, on a set of the project's own questions, `INDEX.md` plus grep makes an agent
   182	   read more to reach the right entry than ranked search would. The B1.1 comparison measures exactly that (EJ,
   183	   2026-10-12: *"the comparison for full-text"*).
   184	5. **Why only as a cache?** The markdown is the source of truth, and the cache must be rebuildable and skippable (M6).
   185	
   186	Structured questions such as "every active decision covering path X" or "what supersedes Y" do not need SQLite. A
   187	short script reading the entry headers answers them in 0.1 s at 10,000 entries (`S-F13`). That measures time, not
   188	tokens; my reading is that the script and an SQL query return the same list of entries, so the agent reads the same
   189	amount either way (not measured).
   190	
   191	### 5W1H
   192	
   193	| | |
   194	|---|---|
   195	| **Who** | Built and rebuilt by a script with no model call. Read by any agent on demand. EJ decides to build it when the trigger fires. |
   196	| **What** | One FTS5 table with `kind` and `covers` as `UNINDEXED` columns (`S-F2`). One row per active entry file: the research leaves the row grain open (`S-F17`); my reading is that an entry of one or two sentences is already one section. The default `unicode61` tokenizer: in the researcher's own one-row test, a plausible change to keep paths whole made ordinary queries return zero rows silently, and ids such as `D-0012` had to be quoted (`S-F16`). |
   197	| **When** | Built when its B1.1 comparison passes and EJ decides (B1.0). Rebuilt before any query that finds it stale. Never read at session start. |
   198	| **Where** | `project-memory/.cache/index.sqlite`, gitignored. |
   199	| **Why** | The five whys above. |
   200	| **How** | One stdlib `sqlite3` script, the smallest option in the SQLite node (§5, option B): rebuild to a temporary file and rename; compare the recorded content hash for staleness, so a deleted or uncommitted change is caught (K1.1, K2.1); fall back to `rg`. Nothing to install: SQLite 3.45.1 with FTS5 works here (verified 2026-10-12). |
   201	
   202	## Vector index
   203	
   204	### Five whys
   205	
   206	1. **Why a vector index?** To find an entry by meaning when the question shares no words with it.
   207	2. **Why can't grep or full-text search do that?** They match words. But the evidence does not show vectors winning on
   208	   memory text. (They do win on some code tasks, `V-F5`, and on half the file-based pairs in `V-F3`.) On conversational
   209	   memory, grep returned inline beat vector search for every harness and model pair (`V-F3`). On a 500,000-document
   210	   corpus, BM25 beat vectors even on the questions built to favour them (`V-F4`). On decision-style records, a
   211	   structured graph and a vector memory tool were "largely equivalent" on relevance, and plain notes search did no
   212	   better on listing or supersession questions (s3-F34; `V-F1`).
   213	3. **Why not build it now?** No measurement exists on a corpus shaped like `entries/` (`V-F11`: not measured).
   214	   Anthropic's 2024 guidance is that under about 200,000 tokens a knowledge base can simply be put in the prompt
   215	   (`V-F8`), roughly 400–650 entries (`V-F12`, my reading). This design does not do that (it never loads the whole
   216	   tree), so this is only a bound: below it, retrieval quality is not what limits an agent (my reading). Vectors also
   217	   add failures of their own: negation (`V-F20`), exact ids and paths (`V-F21`), and a second rebuild step that can lag
   218	   the first (`V-F24`).
   219	4. **Why might it be needed later?** If agents miss entries that exist because their words differ from the entry's.
   220	   The vector index is worth building when, on questions written in words different from their entries, it finds
   221	   entries full-text did not, and it does no worse on the other questions (B1.2).
   222	5. **Why hybrid only?** In the studies found (code and chunk RAG, not memory entries), the positive results combine
   223	   vectors with keyword search (`V-F6`, `V-F7`); on agent memory, `V-F10` is unproven, and vectors alone were the
   224	   weakest arm when results were returned inline (`V-F3`, `V-F4`). So the vector index comes after the full-text index,
   225	   never instead of it. This is a **proposed** policy: on half the file-based pairs in `V-F3` vectors won alone (why 2
   226	   above), so the evidence does not compel it.
   227	
   228	### 5W1H
   229	
   230	| | |
   231	|---|---|
   232	| **Who** | Built and rebuilt by a script with no model call except the embedding model itself. Read by any agent on demand, through the same command as full-text search. EJ decides to build it when the trigger fires. |
   233	| **What** | One vector per active entry file, with the entry's `covers`, content hash and embedding-model id stored beside it (`V-F24`: a changed model makes every vector useless). Results merged with the full-text results by reciprocal rank fusion (vector node §Q5, from qmd's README). |
   234	| **When** | Built when its B1.2 comparison passes and EJ decides (B1.0), after the full-text index exists. Rebuilt with the full-text index, in the same step, so the two cannot disagree (`V-F24`, my reading). |
   235	| **Where** | `project-memory/.cache/`, beside `index.sqlite`, gitignored. |
   236	| **Why** | The five whys above. |
   237	| **How** | The smallest options in `V-F18`/`V-F19`: a static Model2Vec model with numpy (option A, no GPU: its model and runtime; its incremental rebuild is not used, B3.1) or `bge-small-en-v1.5` through FastEmbed (option B). Both are small next to the 16 GB GPU here. Embedding speed on this GPU is **not measured** (`V-F18`). No embedding runtime is installed today; numpy is (`V-F19`). |
   238	
   239	## Measured here: development and testing
   240	
   241	EJ's aim is that development and testing get faster and cheaper in tokens. So this section measures all three stores
   242	against grep on **this repo**: 490 files (123 `.py`, 367 `.md`, 4.8 MB), with 40 questions an agent asks while
   243	developing and testing it. Run: `runs/20261012-caches-routing/` (results in `bench/results.md`; BM-7 is from
   244	`nodes/runtime-recall.json` and the suite timing from the run's `state.md`; the `BM-*` ids are labels given here).
   245	
   246	**Who wrote each check, and what it does not establish.**
   247	- **The corpus moved during the build** (4,016 → 4,017 chunks; the builder: *"Re-run before the tables are used"*). A
   248	  later run on the repaired harness is recorded, but nothing records that the corpus held still. rg's seconds include
   249	  a `git ls-files` call an agent would not pay, about 16% (audit); the speed winner does not change.
   250	- The **questions and answers** were written by codex (`gpt-6.1-sol`), each checked by an `rg` command it ran. Five
   251	  Claude agents re-checked all 40: 37 were correct and 3 were incomplete. Those 3 were completed.
   252	- **"Which tests run this code"** is also scored against per-test runtime coverage (`pytest-cov`, no model). That
   253	  counts in-process Python only, so it misses tests that run the code as a separate process.
   254	- The **harness** was written by opencode. A Claude audit found 5 high-severity biases, and a Claude repair fixed
   255	  them; the repair was **not re-audited**. Rebuilding from scratch gave the same answers on all 180 question-route
   256	  pairs.
   257	- **Token cost** is measured as *characters printed up to the first line that names a right file*. That is a proxy,
   258	  not a token count, and a weak one:
   259	  - it stops at the right *file*, not the right answer;
   260	  - misses are left out, so each route's mean is over a different set of questions;
   261	  - each route prints a different format.
   262	
   263	  No agent was run end to end.
   264	- One repo, one question set, **n = 6 to 10 per category**. None of this is a project-memory corpus.
   265	
   266	**What each route returns, per category** (symbol hit in the top 5 / characters read to the first line that names a
   267	right file):
   268	
   269	| | grep (`rg`) | SQLite full-text | vector | hybrid | code graph |
   270	|---|---|---|---|---|---|

